APX.ru = {"country":{"name":"Russia","tagline":"Imperial sparkling at Abrau-Durso, the Don Cossacks' native reds and a protected, fast-growing domestic industry.","classification":"Since the 2020 law, Russian wine can carry a protected geographical indication (ZGU, roughly an IGP: Kuban, Kuban. Taman Peninsula, Don Valley, Stavropol, Dagestan, Terek Valley, Lower Volga and others) or a protected designation of origin (ZNMP, smaller zones often tied to one estate, especially in Krasnodar Krai). 'Russian wine' requires 100 percent Russian-grown grapes; wine made from imported bulk or concentrate must say so. Russia also lists Crimea and Sevastopol as geographical indications, which Ukraine and most states regard as occupied Ukrainian territory."},"regions":[{"name":"Kuban (Krasnodar)","slug":"kuban-krasnodar","tagline":"The Black Sea coast and Taman peninsula: the heart of modern Russian wine.","ids":["ru-abrau-durso","ru-taman"]},{"name":"Don Valley","slug":"don-valley","tagline":"Cossack stanitsas and native reds.","ids":["ru-don-valley"]},{"name":"Dagestan","slug":"dagestan","tagline":"Caspian vineyards around ancient Derbent.","ids":["ru-dagestan"]}],"items":{"ru-abrau-durso":{"name":"Abrau-Durso","cat":"Wine estate and area","region":"Kuban (Krasnodar)","created":"Imperial estate founded 1870","styles":["sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Riesling, Aligoté","summary":"Abrau-Durso lies near Novorossiysk on the Black Sea, around an emerald lake in hills of marl and limestone. It is Russia's most famous sparkling wine house, with tunnels cut into the hillside for ageing.","terroir":"Marl and limestone; maritime Black Sea climate.","producers":["Abrau-Durso"],"nerd_note":"The estate's tunnels were built following the example of Golitsyn's Novy Svet in Crimea.","rslug":"kuban-krasnodar"},"ru-taman":{"name":"Taman Peninsula","cat":"Wine region","region":"Kuban (Krasnodar)","created":"Kuban subregion","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Krasnostop, Saperavi, Cabernet, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"The Taman peninsula, between the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov, is Russia's largest modern wine zone, with large wineries planted since the 2000s. Hot summers are cooled by winds from both seas.","terroir":"Black earth and marl; flat, windy peninsula between two seas.","producers":["Fanagoria","Kuban-Vino (Chateau Tamagne)","Gai-Kodzor (Anapa)"],"nerd_note":"Fanagoria takes its name from Phanagoria, an ancient Greek colony on the peninsula, where archaeologists have found evidence of ancient winemaking.","rslug":"kuban-krasnodar"},"ru-don-valley":{"name":"Don Valley","cat":"Wine region","region":"Don Valley","created":"Traditional region","styles":["red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tsimlyansky Cherny, Krasnostop Zolotovsky, Sibirkovy, Plechistik","summary":"Along the Don river in the Rostov region, Cossack villages preserved old native grapes. Winters are so cold that vines are traditionally buried under soil to survive.","terroir":"Chalky and sandy riverbank soils; harsh continental winters.","producers":["Vedernikov","Tsimlyanskie Vina"],"nerd_note":"Tsimlyanskoye, a semi-sweet red sparkling wine from the Don, was famous in imperial Russia.","rslug":"don-valley"},"ru-dagestan":{"name":"Dagestan","cat":"Wine region","region":"Dagestan","created":"Traditional region","styles":["white","red","fortified"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli, Agadai, Saperavi","summary":"Dagestan, on the Caspian coast of the North Caucasus, has one of Russia's largest vineyard areas, mostly used for brandy and table grapes, around Derbent and Kizlyar.","terroir":"Caspian lowland and foothills; hot, dry climate.","producers":["Derbent Wine Company","Kizlyar Brandy Factory"],"nerd_note":"Derbent is often called the oldest city in Russia, with fortifications dating back some 1,500 years.","rslug":"dagestan"}}};
